WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Teams make leak prevention measurable through tests, diagnostics, and review.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:18.360
LeakSanitizer, AddressSanitizer, static analysis, compiler warnings, debug allocation tools, and platform diagnostics can expose retained resources earlier than production monitoring alone.

3
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.320
Repeated create-destroy tests are especially useful.

4
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:28.580
A component that appears fine after one run may retain state across many cycles.

5
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:37.800
Tests that create, use, destroy, and repeat can reveal retained ownership in services, libraries, caches, and factories.

6
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.960
Shutdown tests catch missed cleanup.

7
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:51.460
They should exercise callback deregistration, cache eviction, queue draining, session expiration, failed initialization, and cleanup after error paths.

8
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.080
A clean shutdown path is often where unclear ownership becomes visible.

9
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.280
Static analysis and code review can flag ownership mistakes before runtime.

10
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:11.860
Reviewers should ask who owns this resource, how it is released, whether it can be retained by a cycle, what happens on failure, and whether a cache or queue has a boundary.

11
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:18.700
Findings should become tracked engineering defects with owners, fixes, and regression tests.

12
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.640
Suppressions should be documented and revisited.

13
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.980
Leak prevention improves when tool feedback changes the codebase instead of becoming background noise.

14
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.540
Tooling works best when the team agrees on expectations.

15
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:41.120
Decide which leak checks run locally, which run in CI, what failures block release, and how exceptions are reviewed so the signal remains useful.
